1
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:06,350
You should be close to the truth.

2
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,900
Isn't there anything you've realized?

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:14,000
Some secret they wouldn't mind
killing a man to keep.

4
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:22,020
Have you caught on to something?

5
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:26,630
Kayoko Senba is a developmental biologist.

6
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:28,840
And what of it?

7
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:36,890
[Giving birth to you, was my greatest gift.]
[Kaede]

8
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:43,710
You okay?

9
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:50,620
Hey, wait! Please!

10
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:54,000
Look.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,370
You're searching for your nonexistent sister.

12
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:00,560
She exists.

13
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:05,160
Well, she doesn't exist on paper.

14
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:12,650
I can help you find her.

15
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:17,200
But I'll need you to work with me here.

16
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,580
We'll trade information.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,390
I'll tell you what I know.

18
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:29,790
So talk to me, alright?

19
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:40,820
[The hidden truth of 27 years ago]
[The identity of his sister]

20
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,120
Mother, your smoothie.

21
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:57,700
Have a good one.

22
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:03,710
I am Ishimizaki's daughter, Mari.

23
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:07,370
I came to ask about Shiharu Nanase.

24
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:13,670
Thank you, Mari.

25
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:20,080
Thank you.

26
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:26,370
I only wish we could have spoken sooner.

27
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:30,090
No, I...

28
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:32,400
Please, sit.

29
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:35,510
Thank you.

30
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:43,540
I owe you answers. Ask away.

31
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:48,500
What about this man here?

32
00:02:49,610 --> 00:02:52,190
Can't say for sure. He's far away.

33
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:54,650
- I see.
- Damn it!

34
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:55,910
Tada...

35
00:02:55,910 --> 00:03:00,300
How the hell has he evaded
every security camera in sight?

36
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,950
Are you sure you even saw this Ushio guy?

37
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:04,750
Tada.

38
00:03:09,230 --> 00:03:11,870
I'll go search our files again.

39
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,580
Dr. Senba.

40
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,870
May I begin?

41
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:35,750
First, what inspired you to study genetics?

42
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,210
Ah, the usual.

43
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,040
Yes. I'm sure the question is a familiar one.

44
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,000
That, it is.

45
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:57,690
Usually, I tell people a book
in my father's study inspired me.

46
00:03:57,690 --> 00:04:00,380
And while that's no lie,

47
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:07,660
I had grown interested in the topic
long before I knew of the study.

48
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:13,270
<i>As a child, I often spent my summers</i>

49
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:16,950
<i>exploring my grandfather's garden</i>

50
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:20,370
<i>during our family visits to his place.</i>

51
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:33,500
<i>There, I happened to witness a mutant flower.</i>

52
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:42,790
<i>Upon noticing it, I felt an excitement
unlike any I'd experienced before.</i>

53
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:48,840
For that flower was a mistake made by God.

54
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:57,020
I realized then that even God made errors,

55
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:00,370
and felt a sort of sympathy for him.

56
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:06,200
And this inspired your genetic work?

57
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:09,620
It became my raison d'etre.

58
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:17,000
I wished to understand God.
To stand beside him, as an equal.

59
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:23,620
It was the start
to my twisted interest in life.

60
00:05:26,190 --> 00:05:28,480
[Senba]

61
00:05:29,510 --> 00:05:31,240
Come on.

62
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:32,880
Let's go.

63
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,870
Ready? Hold my hand.

64
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:38,090
Okay.

65
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:41,090
- What's for dinner?
- Hamburgers?

66
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:44,540
- I wanna eat potatoes!
- That sounds good, too.

67
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:58,690
Hirama. I'm doing it today.

68
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:01,660
You sure you can handle this alone?

69
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:03,040
Yes.

70
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:06,580
I can't cause you any more trouble.

71
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:27,110
I want to know where Shiharu is.
[log.6]

72
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:29,330
Of course you do.

73
00:06:29,330 --> 00:06:34,300
Shiharu couldn't do much on her own.

74
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:39,040
I don't think she could outrun
an aggressive assailant.

75
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:48,160
Was Shiharu kidnapped by the person
who murdered my father?

76
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:54,200
I hear you took great care of Shiharu.

77
00:06:56,770 --> 00:06:58,210
Well...

78
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:02,510
I wouldn't say I took care of her.

79
00:07:03,350 --> 00:07:06,950
I was just looking after a friend.

80
00:07:09,810 --> 00:07:12,190
Thank you.

81
00:07:12,190 --> 00:07:15,840
If anything, I'm grateful to her.

82
00:07:18,290 --> 00:07:22,340
<i>I'm truly blessed to have known Shii.</i>

83
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,390
Sit.

84
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:37,830
Mari, can you bring us a towel?

85
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:50,850
You're freezing. Want to take a shower?

86
00:07:50,850 --> 00:07:53,210
Right. That's a good idea.

87
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:00,620
Who is that girl?

88
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,450
She's the daughter of a relative.

89
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:06,020
Did we know anyone with a kid her age?

90
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:07,370
Yes, well...

91
00:08:09,230 --> 00:08:14,540
The poor girl just lost both her parents.

92
00:08:16,350 --> 00:08:18,630
So I've decided to take her in.

93
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:23,930
She's a sickly girl,
who likely won't recover.

94
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:28,940
Mari, could you help me look after her?

95
00:08:30,190 --> 00:08:32,400
You want me to take care of her?

96
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:33,500
But I'm no medical expert.

97
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,370
I'll help you along the way.

98
00:08:35,370 --> 00:08:37,190
That's not the issue here.

99
00:08:37,190 --> 00:08:39,750
This is partially for your sake, Mari.

100
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:46,060
I think you two will become fast friends.

101
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:49,770
What?

102
00:08:49,770 --> 00:08:51,310
But...

103
00:08:51,310 --> 00:08:53,500
Besides, you're not busy with work anyway.

104
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:57,510
I mean, I'm not, but...

105
00:09:01,350 --> 00:09:04,100
You'll dry your hair, right?

106
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:08,580
Follow me.

107
00:09:19,150 --> 00:09:20,470
Hm?

108
00:09:21,290 --> 00:09:22,850
Nothing.

109
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:31,160
<i>My father told me she couldn't
leave the house much because she was sick.</i>

110
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:37,940
But on days she was well,
we went on walks at night.

111
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:41,020
It seems you two got along well.

112
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:44,440
We'd always talk about the silliest things.

113
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:49,010
They were precious moments to me.

114
00:09:51,270 --> 00:09:53,080
I understand entirely.

115
00:09:57,320 --> 00:10:02,450
Why did you leave your daughter with us?

116
00:10:08,310 --> 00:10:14,880
I'm willing to accept
whatever truth you have for me.

117
00:10:16,350 --> 00:10:18,750
Just please, give me an answer.

118
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:26,910
Is my father part of the reason?

119
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:33,000
Yes.

120
00:10:34,730 --> 00:10:37,830
The reason I entrusted my daughter
to your family...

121
00:10:40,060 --> 00:10:45,410
Is because Ishimizaki
is also Shiharu's father.

122
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:57,440
Kyoichi Nanase and Akihiko Ishimizaki
were college buds.

123
00:10:57,440 --> 00:11:02,750
Which is how Haruka Nanase
was let into Ishimizaki's lab.

124
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,520
Your point being?

125
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:12,210
The one who reported Haruka Nanase
for trespassing, was Kayoko Senba.

126
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:14,320
Right.

127
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:17,550
Turns out, something does tie
these four together.

128
00:11:30,030 --> 00:11:31,100
Yes?

129
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:33,600
<i>Mother, it's Keita!</i>

130
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:35,270
<i>Keita, he's...</i>

131
00:11:35,270 --> 00:11:37,310
What?

132
00:11:37,310 --> 00:11:39,080
What about Keita?

133
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:42,380
<i>Keita's been kidnapped!</i>

134
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:44,010
What?

135
00:11:44,690 --> 00:11:46,230
<i>This is all my fault.</i>

136
00:11:46,230 --> 00:11:49,640
<i>I was right there, and he just disappeared!</i>

137
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:53,500
<i>A man called while I was searching,</i>

138
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:57,690
<i>and threatened me not to tell the police...</i>

139
00:11:57,690 --> 00:11:59,570
Talk to me.

140
00:11:59,570 --> 00:12:01,530
Is Keita safe?

141
00:12:01,530 --> 00:12:05,400
<i>The man said he would be,
so long as I accept his demands.</i>

142
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:10,540
<i>He wants all your research on Roopkund.</i>

143
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:18,750
- Hey there.
- Hello.

144
00:12:28,750 --> 00:12:30,980
Shinbashi, speaking.

145
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:34,480
Kayoko Senba is acting strange.

146
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:36,690
Can you look into it?

147
00:12:36,690 --> 00:12:38,580
Yes, ma'am.

148
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:57,360
[Ishimizaki Family Grave]

149
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:08,200
Sir, your guest has arrived.

150
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:25,330
What did you mean by that earlier?

151
00:13:25,330 --> 00:13:28,620
Sit, I'll explain everything.

152
00:13:29,270 --> 00:13:30,750
Alright.

153
00:13:48,350 --> 00:13:52,520
Is Shiharu really my father's daughter?

154
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:55,950
How about something to drink first?

155
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:07,910
Shiharu...

156
00:14:08,990 --> 00:14:14,420
She is a child between myself, Ishimizaki,

157
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:19,160
and Kayoko Senba.

158
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:27,690
Kyoichi Nanase, Akihiko Ishimizaki,
and Kayoko Senba

159
00:14:27,690 --> 00:14:34,750
were all researching human remains
at Roopkund 27 years ago.

160
00:15:04,230 --> 00:15:05,770
It's a success!

161
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:09,210
<i>Something's up between those three.</i>

162
00:15:09,870 --> 00:15:14,010
I reckon Haruka Nanase
is also looking into it.

163
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:10,480
These are the ledgers
from companies who assisted with "log.0".

164
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:13,560
- Thank you.
- Was there anything left?

165
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:18,520
No. I made sure any trace of money
no longer leads back to us.

166
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:22,790
If there is any more evidence on "log.0",
I'll make sure to erase it myself.

167
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:23,680
Show yourself.

168
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:26,640
I know it's you.

169
00:17:33,690 --> 00:17:36,270
Where is Keita? Give him back.

170
00:17:36,270 --> 00:17:39,010
First, I have questions to ask you.

171
00:17:40,040 --> 00:17:42,970
Keita is not here, but he is safe.

172
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:50,230
Can you promise me that?

173
00:17:50,230 --> 00:17:51,620
Yes.

174
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:58,520
You, my father, and Professor Ishimizaki.

175
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:02,980
I want to know what research
you three were doing for Nichie and Plantae,

176
00:18:02,980 --> 00:18:05,710
and how it all ties back to Shiharu.

177
00:18:07,370 --> 00:18:09,430
"Shiharu"?

178
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:12,540
My younger sister.

179
00:18:16,750 --> 00:18:19,390
Professor Ishimizaki sent you an email.

180
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:24,940
He said he was prepared to atone.

181
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:28,980
The Plantae revere Roopkund,
and call it the Land of Souls.

182
00:18:28,980 --> 00:18:35,120
The very same Roopkund
you three were researching.

183
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:38,960
And...

184
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:43,000
There is something my sister once said.

185
00:18:45,260 --> 00:18:47,570
This really is a beautiful place.

186
00:18:47,570 --> 00:18:52,540
In May, the local elementary schoolers come
to do plant cuttings here.

187
00:18:54,310 --> 00:18:56,090
- Plant cutting?
- Yeah.

188
00:18:57,400 --> 00:19:01,510
They replant the stem of a hydrangea,
and it grows into a new one.

189
00:19:05,810 --> 00:19:07,830
Did the stairs tire you?

190
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:12,040
These hydrangeas...

191
00:19:15,090 --> 00:19:17,500
They're the same as me.

192
00:19:22,390 --> 00:19:24,200
Shiharu...

193
00:19:28,150 --> 00:19:30,090
was a plant cutting.

194
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:38,160
Herbert John Webber, a plant physiologist,

195
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:41,640
used another, more commonly known term

196
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:44,790
to refer to plant cuttings.

197
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:52,010
He called them, "clones".

198
00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:59,950
The research you three
immersed yourselves in,

199
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:04,180
was the revival of the dead.

200
00:20:11,350 --> 00:20:15,950
Shiharu Nanase, who grew up
as Kyoichi Nanase's daughter,

201
00:20:20,020 --> 00:20:26,000
is an asexually reproduced clone, isn't she?

202
00:20:28,250 --> 00:20:30,540
That clone...

203
00:20:32,670 --> 00:20:35,100
It was successfully born?

204
00:20:41,980 --> 00:20:49,960
You all are former believers
of Plantae, correct?

205
00:20:52,490 --> 00:20:56,900
Then as I briefly alluded to earlier,

206
00:20:56,900 --> 00:21:03,770
please tell us what occurred in Plantae,
27 years ago.

207
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:08,840
This man here will
pay you for your time today.

208
00:21:09,940 --> 00:21:14,410
Your reward will depend
on how much you tell us.

209
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:18,600
You didn't know about Shiharu?

210
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:21,590
I was kept in the complete dark.

211
00:21:21,590 --> 00:21:24,130
How involved in all this are you?

212
00:21:24,130 --> 00:21:26,660
I said, I know nothing.

213
00:21:28,860 --> 00:21:34,160
Can you even create a clone
from bones as old as those in Roopkund?

214
00:21:35,910 --> 00:21:41,350
If, theoretically speaking,
you were to create a human clone,

215
00:21:41,350 --> 00:21:46,220
you'd have to take the nucleus from a cell,
and inject it into an egg cell.

216
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:49,040
Then transplant that cell
into a host to give birth.

217
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:52,020
But...

218
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:55,600
But that's only theoretically speaking.

219
00:21:55,600 --> 00:21:59,020
We've barely succeeded cloning mice,
but humans? That's...

220
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:00,830
Possible.

221
00:22:02,460 --> 00:22:09,380
The greatest challenge was using
the deteriorating nuclei from a dead body.

222
00:22:11,730 --> 00:22:14,210
How did you resolve that issue?

223
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,060
<i>I developed a method
of repairing the nucleus,</i>

224
00:22:17,060 --> 00:22:21,800
<i>by repeatedly transplanting nuclei
into egg cells, and initiating repairs there.</i>

225
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:25,520
<i>What? A complete repair would be impossible.</i>

226
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:28,270
<i>Each egg cell would only repair a section.</i>

227
00:22:28,270 --> 00:22:31,310
<i>I'd then take that
partially repaired nucleus,</i>

228
00:22:31,310 --> 00:22:35,370
<i>and transplant it into another egg cell
for further repairs.</i>

229
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:37,940
Repeat that over and over,

230
00:22:37,940 --> 00:22:42,340
in a set amount of time, and voilà.

231
00:22:43,350 --> 00:22:45,780
But some damage still remained.

232
00:22:45,780 --> 00:22:49,960
Without my knowledge,
we wouldn't even have results.

233
00:22:49,960 --> 00:22:52,810
If I weren't on the team,
there wouldn't be a baby.

234
00:22:55,240 --> 00:22:59,370
What happened to the clone?

235
00:23:03,980 --> 00:23:06,260
You refuse to tell me.

236
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:10,890
Who ordered you to do this?

237
00:23:11,770 --> 00:23:15,000
My father? Plantae?

238
00:23:17,770 --> 00:23:22,900
It all began when my gene therapy research

239
00:23:22,900 --> 00:23:26,730
was cut short by religious groups in the UK.

240
00:23:26,730 --> 00:23:29,620
<i>I had returned to Japan, hoping for more.</i>

241
00:23:37,210 --> 00:23:38,690
Yes?

242
00:23:38,690 --> 00:23:41,560
You must be Dr. Kayoko Senba.

243
00:23:43,070 --> 00:23:45,330
And you are?

244
00:23:46,090 --> 00:23:49,500
I would like your help
in reviving our Holy One.

245
00:23:52,850 --> 00:23:55,750
Who is this "Holy One" you're referring to?

246
00:23:57,250 --> 00:24:03,030
Sojiro Manabe. The founder of Plantae.

247
00:24:03,030 --> 00:24:05,160
Plantae?

248
00:24:06,980 --> 00:24:10,700
What does a religious group like yours want
from a scientist like myself?

249
00:24:11,310 --> 00:24:14,500
Before Brother Sojiro Manabe passed away,

250
00:24:15,060 --> 00:24:18,550
he claimed men would not inherit the Plantae.

251
00:24:20,030 --> 00:24:24,230
Rather, it would be a Holy Woman
revived from the Land of Souls

252
00:24:24,230 --> 00:24:30,040
who would unite all life on Earth.

253
00:24:30,770 --> 00:24:37,660
<i>He wanted me to unearth
the remains of a woman from Roopkund,</i>

254
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:42,270
<i>and replicate her.</i>

255
00:24:42,270 --> 00:24:46,750
You want me to create a clone
from an ancient skeleton?

256
00:24:46,750 --> 00:24:48,080
Yes.

257
00:24:49,060 --> 00:24:52,420
I want you to create us a Holy Woman.

258
00:24:54,900 --> 00:24:57,060
You're asking the impossible.

259
00:24:57,060 --> 00:25:00,450
You can't create a person from dead cells.

260
00:25:06,440 --> 00:25:08,550
I'm relieved.

261
00:25:09,730 --> 00:25:14,830
A normal scientist would first decline
by bringing up ethics.

262
00:25:16,930 --> 00:25:21,690
If the issue is the necessary skill
and technology, Plantae will assist,

263
00:25:21,690 --> 00:25:26,330
as well as Nichie Pharmaceutical's
Yumihiko Nanase.

264
00:25:27,370 --> 00:25:29,590
Even so...

265
00:25:31,100 --> 00:25:37,770
God tested Brother Manabe,
and left him unable to produce a child.

266
00:25:39,480 --> 00:25:42,550
This is our only way.

267
00:25:42,550 --> 00:25:47,500
You ought to have created a clone
while he was still alive.

268
00:25:49,950 --> 00:25:51,850
We did.

269
00:25:56,440 --> 00:26:01,420
However, Brother Manabe was irreplaceable.

270
00:26:03,060 --> 00:26:05,750
No one could accept I was his successor.

271
00:26:12,680 --> 00:26:14,540
How about it, Dr. Senba?

272
00:26:21,490 --> 00:26:25,580
<i>"This man sitting in front of me is a clone?"</i>

273
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:30,540
At first, I couldn't believe him.

274
00:26:30,540 --> 00:26:36,510
But if he were telling the truth...

275
00:26:38,060 --> 00:26:40,600
I could overcome any sort of boundary,

276
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:44,580
and pursue my research
with as many resources as I wished.

277
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:49,510
That is an opportunity
every researcher dreams of.

278
00:26:54,100 --> 00:26:59,180
Won't you join us, in creating a God?

279
00:27:04,490 --> 00:27:06,420
<i>No.</i>

280
00:27:11,310 --> 00:27:15,830
<i>For I would become God.</i>

281
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:21,480
So you created a human being.

282
00:27:23,650 --> 00:27:25,810
<i>It was ironic.</i>

283
00:27:25,810 --> 00:27:31,060
<i>Overseas, religious views on ethics
hindered scientific breakthroughs.</i>

284
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:37,180
<i>While in Japan, religion overcame
such ethical boundaries with ease.</i>

285
00:27:38,730 --> 00:27:44,180
You people had to find a host
who would give birth to the clone.

286
00:27:44,180 --> 00:27:46,810
How did you choose the mother?

287
00:27:46,810 --> 00:27:49,370
Did Plantae offer some of their people?

288
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:52,060
Indeed.

289
00:27:52,060 --> 00:27:56,100
They gathered young women?

290
00:27:56,100 --> 00:27:57,810
Yes.

291
00:27:57,810 --> 00:28:01,980
They said Brother Manabe
needed our assistance,

292
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:06,380
so female worshippers in their twenties
signed up for a health checkup.

293
00:28:07,190 --> 00:28:12,200
Was an employee from Nichie
in charge of these checkups?

294
00:28:14,190 --> 00:28:18,020
We do not know who the doctor was.

295
00:28:18,020 --> 00:28:23,810
But an individual from Nichie
was overseeing the whole process.

296
00:28:23,810 --> 00:28:27,750
30 women signed up for the exam.

297
00:28:27,750 --> 00:28:32,190
They wanted a woman
who would carry Brother Manabe's child!

298
00:28:32,190 --> 00:28:37,400
I thought Brother Manabe
had passed away by this time.

299
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:40,230
I believe he had his sperm frozen.

300
00:28:40,230 --> 00:28:43,730
The girl who was chosen was overjoyed.

301
00:28:43,730 --> 00:28:46,260
She was so grateful to be of use.

302
00:28:47,230 --> 00:28:51,270
It was like watching a sacrifice
be blind with happiness.

303
00:28:51,270 --> 00:28:54,810
The way they treated women
as mere birth-giving vessels

304
00:28:54,810 --> 00:28:58,290
had me become disillusioned
with Plantae as a whole.

305
00:29:00,120 --> 00:29:03,520
What did you tell those chosen five?

306
00:29:03,520 --> 00:29:06,100
Did you tell them about the clone?

307
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:08,990
Of course not.

308
00:29:08,990 --> 00:29:12,310
Just that we needed their assistance
in creating a Holy Woman.

309
00:29:12,310 --> 00:29:16,110
At least, that's how the Plantae organizer
explained it.

310
00:29:16,110 --> 00:29:19,450
And who was this organizer?

311
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:24,900
The current Head of PR,

312
00:29:24,900 --> 00:29:30,410
<i>Kasuga. She practically runs
the organization at this point.</i>

313
00:29:33,650 --> 00:29:39,800
As a result of our experiment,
one woman successfully took the cell.

314
00:29:40,900 --> 00:29:43,910
My mother.

315
00:29:47,060 --> 00:29:49,800
<i>Kaede Tozaki.</i>

316
00:29:51,110 --> 00:29:53,170
<i>She was my mother.</i>

317
00:30:02,490 --> 00:30:04,630
I can't believe this.

318
00:30:06,540 --> 00:30:11,750
You're saying Shiharu
was an artificially-made human being?

319
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:19,930
Was this all your doing?

320
00:30:20,810 --> 00:30:26,040
The project began with my father.

321
00:30:27,340 --> 00:30:33,020
Back then,
I was desperate for his approval.

322
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:35,540
I clearly couldn't see my surroundings.

323
00:30:37,980 --> 00:30:40,760
I even got Ishimizaki involved.

324
00:30:45,720 --> 00:30:49,410
Why?

325
00:30:52,020 --> 00:30:55,260
Why would you rope my father into this?

326
00:30:59,450 --> 00:31:03,040
Isn't that why he died?

327
00:31:05,150 --> 00:31:08,580
Why he was murdered?

328
00:31:10,310 --> 00:31:15,080
I don't know.

329
00:31:16,980 --> 00:31:22,430
But I deeply regret getting him involved.

330
00:31:22,990 --> 00:31:26,810
He was the only person I trusted.

331
00:31:29,560 --> 00:31:31,330
I'm sorry.

332
00:31:35,420 --> 00:31:37,330
I'm so sorry.

333
00:31:54,950 --> 00:31:57,330
And are you okay?

334
00:31:58,520 --> 00:32:00,160
Yes.

335
00:32:01,060 --> 00:32:03,040
Where is Shii?

336
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:07,390
Where is Shiharu?

337
00:32:10,310 --> 00:32:12,450
She's safe, right?

338
00:32:13,150 --> 00:32:15,270
Please, tell me the truth.

339
00:32:15,270 --> 00:32:17,540
I want to see her again!

340
00:32:20,160 --> 00:32:21,960
Shiharu is...

341
00:32:27,190 --> 00:32:29,580
Shiharu is dead.

342
00:32:39,060 --> 00:32:41,000
My mother died.

343
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:43,970
I see.

344
00:32:47,850 --> 00:32:49,620
That's it?

345
00:32:51,690 --> 00:32:57,000
You're thinking she died
because she gave birth to a clone?

346
00:33:02,310 --> 00:33:04,750
Can you prove me wrong?

347
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,660
One of Plantae's executives.

348
00:33:24,660 --> 00:33:26,600
Why is she here?

349
00:33:26,600 --> 00:33:29,450
The girl next to her is my junior.

350
00:33:33,140 --> 00:33:35,620
Then she's a fellow devotee.

351
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:39,700
Everyone's connected somehow.

352
00:33:44,980 --> 00:33:46,700
Sir?

353
00:33:46,700 --> 00:33:49,520
What are you doing? Follow your orders.

354
00:33:49,520 --> 00:33:53,610
I'm compiling my findings for you, Chief.

355
00:33:53,610 --> 00:33:57,750
Ishimizaki, Kyoichi Nanase,
and Senba Kayoko were at Roopkund...

356
00:33:57,750 --> 00:33:59,700
Digging into that isn't your job.

357
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:01,150
But...

358
00:34:01,150 --> 00:34:03,660
Just do the work you've been given.

359
00:34:05,810 --> 00:34:07,960
That's how an organization keeps functioning.

360
00:34:12,810 --> 00:34:15,230
What did he need?

361
00:34:15,230 --> 00:34:17,210
The higher-ups want me to stop.

362
00:34:17,210 --> 00:34:19,560
I'm not surprised,

363
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:23,230
since you aren't following orders.

364
00:34:23,230 --> 00:34:26,080
Something's up with Roopkund.

365
00:34:31,190 --> 00:34:36,310
Ishimizaki blamed himself.

366
00:34:36,310 --> 00:34:39,630
So he felt guilty about my mother.

367
00:34:40,790 --> 00:34:43,660
After the passing
of the previous head of Nichie,

368
00:34:43,660 --> 00:34:47,100
your step-grandfather, Yumihiko Nanase,

369
00:34:47,100 --> 00:34:50,350
the clone project

370
00:34:50,350 --> 00:34:54,620
was entirely called off.

371
00:34:55,810 --> 00:34:59,020
If that's enough, then return Keita to me.

372
00:34:59,020 --> 00:35:03,040
Professor Ishimizaki wanted to go public
with the truth, didn't he?

373
00:35:04,050 --> 00:35:06,040
I was entirely against it.

374
00:35:06,040 --> 00:35:07,310
Is that why you killed him?

375
00:35:07,310 --> 00:35:08,480
I did no such thing.

376
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:10,040
Then was it my father?

377
00:35:17,190 --> 00:35:20,940
I received Ishimizaki's email about atoning

378
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:24,750
several days before he was killed.

379
00:35:26,520 --> 00:35:29,940
I thought he planned to
make our findings public.

380
00:35:29,940 --> 00:35:32,040
How did you reply to him?

381
00:35:33,310 --> 00:35:37,160
I had planned to see him that day.

382
00:35:41,810 --> 00:35:45,870
<i>I wanted to talk,
before he did anything rash.</i>

383
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:57,000
Ishimizaki?

384
00:36:15,060 --> 00:36:17,540
Ishimizaki?

385
00:36:31,150 --> 00:36:32,750
You...

386
00:36:40,210 --> 00:36:44,040
<i>I think he was killed
because he tried to reveal our secret.</i>

387
00:36:45,490 --> 00:36:47,830
That should be enough.

388
00:36:48,900 --> 00:36:50,790
Where is Keita?

389
00:36:51,450 --> 00:36:53,620
Hand me your findings first.

390
00:37:06,190 --> 00:37:10,950
This is what killed Ishimizaki.

391
00:37:12,420 --> 00:37:15,120
If you make it public, I will be...

392
00:37:16,020 --> 00:37:17,580
You won't be safe, either.

393
00:37:18,070 --> 00:37:20,250
I won't be making it public.

394
00:37:38,060 --> 00:37:41,060
Keita is already with Tomoe.

395
00:37:41,660 --> 00:37:43,850
Wow!

396
00:37:43,850 --> 00:37:46,650
- Again!
- Again?

397
00:37:46,650 --> 00:37:47,940
You speedster.

398
00:37:47,940 --> 00:37:52,500
Great job!

399
00:37:53,900 --> 00:37:55,750
I see.

400
00:37:57,150 --> 00:37:59,670
You got Tomoe on your side.

401
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:04,000
That woman...

402
00:38:05,810 --> 00:38:08,750
You have no right to condemn Tomoe.

403
00:38:08,750 --> 00:38:10,750
You killed my mother.

404
00:38:10,750 --> 00:38:14,980
Your mother understood the risks.

405
00:38:14,980 --> 00:38:16,940
Nothing was guaranteed.

406
00:38:16,940 --> 00:38:20,340
We even gave her a thorough explanation.

407
00:38:20,340 --> 00:38:23,670
You people took advantage of her faith.

408
00:38:23,670 --> 00:38:26,620
You knew worshippers
would do anything for the organization.

409
00:38:26,620 --> 00:38:28,950
If she was faithful, so be it.

410
00:38:28,950 --> 00:38:30,830
Quit avoiding the main issue here.

411
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:36,490
I see.

412
00:38:37,530 --> 00:38:41,530
I thought you would be more understanding.

413
00:38:41,530 --> 00:38:43,650
What a shame.

414
00:38:43,650 --> 00:38:45,450
Oh, I understand.

415
00:38:46,670 --> 00:38:52,190
You're frustrated that your accomplishments
aren't seeing the light of day.

416
00:38:52,190 --> 00:38:56,190
You could never understand
the possibilities I was denied.

417
00:38:56,190 --> 00:38:58,750
Playing the victim, huh?

418
00:38:58,750 --> 00:39:00,190
Do you even feel guilt?

419
00:39:00,190 --> 00:39:02,310
What more could I have done?

420
00:39:02,310 --> 00:39:05,660
So you'll instead forget
the humans you've used.

421
00:39:08,290 --> 00:39:12,540
You could never understand.

422
00:39:13,730 --> 00:39:18,000
I hope I never do.
Because I wish to remain human.

423
00:39:20,690 --> 00:39:24,840
And you. You're no god.

424
00:39:28,690 --> 00:39:30,900
I was told

425
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:37,250
your sister was a stillbirth.

426
00:39:38,860 --> 00:39:43,340
Your father and Ishimizaki
hid her existence from me.

427
00:39:44,360 --> 00:39:46,580
Why do you think that is?

428
00:39:48,230 --> 00:39:54,410
Before venting at me,
you ought to check your surroundings first.

429
00:40:08,100 --> 00:40:09,770
- Please come again.
- Thank you.

430
00:40:09,770 --> 00:40:12,240
Thank you.

431
00:40:13,400 --> 00:40:16,060
I'll have my chauffeur drive us home.

432
00:40:16,060 --> 00:40:18,350
I'd like to think on this alone.

433
00:40:18,350 --> 00:40:19,940
Then I'll call a taxi for you.

434
00:40:19,940 --> 00:40:22,040
I'd prefer walking home.

435
00:40:24,020 --> 00:40:30,220
Even I don't know what exactly is going on.

436
00:40:30,220 --> 00:40:32,620
Please refrain from doing anything dangerous.

437
00:40:33,780 --> 00:40:35,620
Okay.

438
00:40:35,620 --> 00:40:40,140
And you won't look into anything unnecessary?

439
00:40:41,310 --> 00:40:42,790
I won't.

440
00:40:44,370 --> 00:40:47,020
Good. Take care.

441
00:40:47,020 --> 00:40:50,830
Thank you.

442
00:41:11,770 --> 00:41:14,100
- Shii.
- Hm?

443
00:41:14,100 --> 00:41:16,580
Is there anything you want to do
when you're older?

444
00:41:18,850 --> 00:41:23,040
I don't know.
I don't even live a normal life.

445
00:41:23,950 --> 00:41:25,470
What about you?

446
00:41:25,470 --> 00:41:27,410
Me?

447
00:41:27,410 --> 00:41:30,020
I don't have any lofty dreams.

448
00:41:30,020 --> 00:41:33,080
- You don't?
- Nope.

449
00:41:34,850 --> 00:41:38,560
But I guess I'd want to be hired
at a sizable company.

450
00:41:38,560 --> 00:41:42,520
Somewhere that'd let me get off work on time.

451
00:41:42,520 --> 00:41:46,230
A place that's only an hour train ride away
from Dad's place.

452
00:41:46,230 --> 00:41:49,600
And when I visit my usual store
on my way home from work,

453
00:41:49,600 --> 00:41:52,330
I'd know all the regulars there.

454
00:41:53,110 --> 00:41:55,150
- Sounds like a nice future.
- Right?

455
00:41:55,150 --> 00:41:58,700
The store has a delicious menu,

456
00:41:58,700 --> 00:42:03,080
and all the regulars taste test new dishes,

457
00:42:03,080 --> 00:42:05,850
and give the chef our honest reviews.

458
00:42:05,850 --> 00:42:08,080
It's like you're talking from experience.

459
00:42:09,520 --> 00:42:12,920
The store down there was especially good.

460
00:42:13,730 --> 00:42:15,940
And now you are?

461
00:42:15,940 --> 00:42:18,840
Yup. Since I never had a mom,

462
00:42:18,840 --> 00:42:23,600
I used to meet Dad there for dinner.

463
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:27,580
We ate there practically every single day.

464
00:42:28,330 --> 00:42:30,120
Sounds nice.

465
00:42:30,120 --> 00:42:33,190
So even as a kid,
I'd say my favorite food was minced fish.

466
00:42:33,190 --> 00:42:35,380
None of the other kids could relate.

467
00:42:35,380 --> 00:42:37,200
This is the store.

468
00:42:38,440 --> 00:42:40,730
It closed down?

469
00:42:40,730 --> 00:42:45,250
Yeah. Competition built up and all that.

470
00:42:46,010 --> 00:42:47,830
- I see.
- Yeah.

471
00:42:51,100 --> 00:42:54,500
I've got it. My dream.

472
00:42:54,500 --> 00:42:55,810
What is it?

473
00:42:55,810 --> 00:42:58,150
We'll run a store together.

474
00:42:58,150 --> 00:43:00,690
One that serves delicious minced fish.

475
00:43:05,310 --> 00:43:06,950
Sounds fun.

476
00:43:07,750 --> 00:43:09,260
You okay? Are you tired?

477
00:43:09,260 --> 00:43:11,580
I'm fine.

478
00:43:12,440 --> 00:43:14,710
I want to walk a bit longer.

479
00:43:15,520 --> 00:43:17,000
Okay.

480
00:43:57,120 --> 00:43:59,080
What are you doing here?

481
00:44:01,310 --> 00:44:06,470
Sir. Isn't there something
you've been hiding from me?

482
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:10,980
This is Nanase, speaking.

483
00:44:10,980 --> 00:44:14,850
Yes. I've finished speaking with her.

484
00:44:14,850 --> 00:44:16,500
Yes.

485
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:20,950
<i>She realized I was helping you, didn't she?</i>

486
00:44:22,070 --> 00:44:24,610
I would've loved to see her face.

487
00:44:28,270 --> 00:44:32,040
It's fine. I won't be returning home anyway.

488
00:44:32,650 --> 00:44:38,160
The next time I'll see her
will either be the divorce, or in court.

489
00:44:39,150 --> 00:44:42,000
I'll be living in a hotel for a while.

490
00:44:43,650 --> 00:44:45,940
Thank you, really.

491
00:44:45,940 --> 00:44:50,020
No, thank you.

492
00:44:50,020 --> 00:44:51,580
Take care.

493
00:44:55,020 --> 00:44:56,240
Is that good?

494
00:44:56,240 --> 00:44:58,770
- Yeah!
- Then is that your dinner?

495
00:44:58,770 --> 00:45:01,620
- No!
- Thought so.

496
00:45:10,980 --> 00:45:12,810
Hello.

497
00:45:36,560 --> 00:45:39,960
I thought I told you to stay out of this.

498
00:45:42,750 --> 00:45:47,040
I have something to tell you
about the human you created.

499
00:45:51,610 --> 00:45:56,130
The girl from Roopkund is alive.
